@@ -23,3 +23,31 @@ It throws an `Error` when the pointer is not valid RFC 6901 syntax, or when it d
 missing member, an array index out of range (`-` is never a valid index here), or a step into a
 scalar. Only an object's own members are resolved, so inherited names such as `constructor` throw.
 Resolution is a read-only lookup, so the document is never modified.
+
+### JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies a JSON Patch, an array of operations, to a JSON document and
+returns the patched document. The six operations are `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and
+`test`, and they take their JSON Pointers as described in RFC 6901 above.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+const document = { orders: [{ id: "A-1" }] };
+applyPatch(document, [
+  { op: "add", path: "/orders/-", value: { id: "A-2" } },
+  { op: "replace", path: "/orders/0/id", value: "A-9" },
+  { op: "test", path: "/orders/0/id", value: "A-9" },
+]);
+// { orders: [{ id: "A-9" }, { id: "A-2" }] }
+```
+
+Operations run in order, and each one sees the results of the ones before it. The patch is validated
+in full before any operation runs. It throws an `Error` when the patch is not a valid JSON Patch, for
+example an unknown `op`, a missing `value` or a `path` that is not a JSON Pointer, and when an
+operation fails: a `test` whose value does not match, a location that does not exist, an array index
+out of range, an attempt to remove the whole document, or a move into one of its own children.
+
+The input document is never modified. The result is a new value that shares no object or array with
+the input or with the patch, so the caller can change it without affecting either. Values are compared
+by content in `test`: member order in an object does not matter, element order in an array does.
